            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics (author-hosted Cambridge lecture notes)",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S1"
            },
            {
              "title": "Hans-Otto Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction, Probability Theory and Related Fields 99 (1994), 171–195",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "Nonrelativistic classical point-particle models whose thermodynamic claims concern positions and pair interactions; kinetic momentum variables are outside this definition.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-td-admissible-van-hove-and-tempered-exterior",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Van Hove regions and uniformly tempered exterior preparations",
        "statement": "Use the dimensionless coordinates, simple configurations, and pair potential of [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]]. A sequence $(\\Lambda_k)_{k\\geq1}$ of bounded measurable regions is van Hove if $|\\Lambda_k|\\to\\infty$ and, for every fixed $s>0$,\n$$\\frac{|\\{x\\in\\Lambda_k:\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\partial\\Lambda_k)\\leq s\\}|}{|\\Lambda_k|}\\longrightarrow0.$$\nThis is an explicit condition on the boundary-layer volume; increasing volume alone does not imply it. A free wall permits particles only in $\\Lambda_k$ and includes no exterior particles. A periodic wall uses the specified periodic cell and its image convention.\n\nFor the deterministic exterior class, let $C=[-1/2,1/2)^d$ and $\\Lambda_n=[-n-1/2,n+1/2)^d$, with $V_n=|\\Lambda_n|$. For a locally finite exterior configuration $\\zeta\\subset\\mathbb R^d$, write $N_{C+i}(\\zeta)=|\\zeta\\cap(C+i)|$. Given $t<\\infty$, define\n$$\\Omega(t)=\\left\\{\\zeta:\\sum_{i\\in\\Lambda_n\\cap\\mathbb Z^d}N_{C+i}(\\zeta)^2\\leq tV_n\\ \\text{for every integer }n\\geq0\\right\\}.$$\nFor an interior configuration $\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_n$, its exterior interaction is\n$$W_n(\\omega,\\zeta)=\\sum_{x\\in\\omega}\\sum_{y\\in\\zeta\\setminus\\Lambda_n}\\varphi(x-y),$$\ninterpreted as an extended-real sum. A positive infinite value has zero Boltzmann weight. The regular tail controls distant terms; the class $\\Omega(t)$ is a fixed uniform bound on exterior cell counts, not permission for an arbitrary volume-filling external potential. The van Hove and uniformly tempered exterior conditions are separate boundary regimes.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Hans-Otto Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction, Probability Theory and Related Fields 99 (1994), 171–195",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Ruelle, Superstable Interactions in Classical Statistical Mechanics, Communications in Mathematical Physics 18 (1970), 127–159",
              "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~ruelle/PUBLICATIONS/%5B28%5D.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "Classical positional pair-potential models in dimension d at least two, with free walls, periodic walls, or the explicitly bounded deterministic exterior class defined here.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Indistinguishable-particle positional measure",
        "statement": "Let $\\Lambda\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be a bounded Lebesgue-measurable region in the dimensionless coordinates of [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]]. Use the completed product-Lebesgue sigma-algebra on $\\Lambda^N$. For $N\\geq1$, let\n$$D_{\\Lambda,N}=\\{(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)\\in\\Lambda^N:x_i\\ne x_j\\text{ whenever }i\\ne j\\},\\qquad \\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,N}=D_{\\Lambda,N}/S_N,$$\nwhere the permutation group $S_N$ acts by relabelling coordinates. Give $\\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,N}$ the quotient sigma-algebra: $A\\subseteq\\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,N}$ is measurable exactly when its preimage under the quotient map $q_N:D_{\\Lambda,N}\\to\\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,N}$ is measurable in the trace of the completed product-Lebesgue sigma-algebra. Identify each orbit with its unordered simple configuration. For $N=0$, set $\\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,0}=\\{\\varnothing\\}$. If $\\lambda_{\\Lambda}^{\\otimes N}$ is product Lebesgue measure restricted to $D_{\\Lambda,N}$, define the sector reference measure by\n$$\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}=\\frac1{N!}(q_N)_\\#\\lambda_{\\Lambda}^{\\otimes N}\\quad(N\\geq1),\\qquad \\mu_{\\Lambda,0}(\\{\\varnothing\\})=1.$$\nEquivalently, for every nonnegative measurable function $f$ on $\\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,N}$,\n$$\\int f(\\omega)\\,\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega)=\\frac1{N!}\\int_{D_{\\Lambda,N}}f(q_N(x_1,\\ldots,x_N))\\,d^{dN}x.$$\nCollision diagonals have product Lebesgue measure zero, so $\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(\\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,N})=|\\Lambda|^N/N!$; in particular, symmetric integrands may use the equivalent integral over all of $\\Lambda^N$. Thus $\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}$ is a finite measure, not generally a probability. Two labelled tuples represent the same state exactly when they are in one $S_N$ orbit. The factor $1/N!$ is an adopted indistinguishable-particle positional convention, not a theorem about classical trajectories or every classical description.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C_\\Lambda=\\bigsqcup_{N\\geq0}\\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,N}$ with the disjoint-union sigma-algebra, and regard each $\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}$ as a measure on this union supported on its $N$-point sector. Define the unit-intensity finite-region Poisson reference by\n$$Q_\\Lambda=e^{-|\\Lambda|}\\sum_{N=0}^{\\infty}\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}.$$\nIts total mass is one because $\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(\\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,N})=|\\Lambda|^N/N!$ and $e^{-|\\Lambda|}\\sum_{N\\geq0}|\\Lambda|^N/N!=1$. This is a positional reference measure in the chosen dimensionless coordinates; it supplies no empirical claim about particle trajectories.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics (author-hosted Cambridge lecture notes)",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S1"
            },
            {
              "title": "Konstantin K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part SM: Statistical Mechanics (2024 author-hosted lecture notes)",
              "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Essential_Graduate_Physics_-_Statistical_Mechanics_%28Likharev%29/03%3A_Ideal_and_Not-So-Ideal_Gases/3.01%3A_Ideal_classical_gas"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "Classical models of identical point particles of one species represented only by their positions in a fixed finite spatial region; it does not assert quantum statistics or include momentum states.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-td-controlled-fixed-n-energy-shell",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Exact-particle-number positional energy shell",
        "statement": "Use $\\Lambda_n=[-n-\\tfrac12,n+\\tfrac12)^d$, its side length $L_n=2n+1$ and dimensionless volume $V_n=L_n^d$, the periodic positional energy $H_{L_n,\\mathrm{per}}$ of [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]], and the exact-$N$ measure $\\mu_{\\Lambda_n,N}$ of [[post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure]]. Given $N_n\\in\\mathbb N_0$, a reduced configuration-energy center $e\\in\\mathbb R$ joules per dimensionless reference volume, and a width $\\delta_n>0$ in the same units, define\n$$S_n(N_n,e,\\delta_n)=\\{\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_n:|\\omega|=N_n,\\ |H_{L_n,\\mathrm{per}}(\\omega)/V_n-e|\\leq\\delta_n\\}.$$\nWhen $0<\\mu_{\\Lambda_n,N_n}(S_n)<\\infty$, the uniform positional shell law is\n$$M_n(A)=\\frac{\\mu_{\\Lambda_n,N_n}(A\\cap S_n)}{\\mu_{\\Lambda_n,N_n}(S_n)}.$$\nA shrinking energy-density shell uses some specified sequence $\\delta_n\\downarrow0$. Its total energy width is $2\\delta_nV_n$, which is $o(V_n)$ but need not be microscopic or bounded. Existence of a normalizing shell and the existence of a suitable shrinking sequence are separate theorem obligations. This is not a surface measure on an exact-energy hypersurface and not a phase-space shell for $K(p)+H_N^{\\mathrm{per}}$.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation",
          "post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Hans-Otto Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction, Probability Theory and Related Fields 99 (1994), 171–195",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics (author-hosted Cambridge lecture notes)",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S1"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "Finite periodic boxes of the classical pair-potential positional model; shells constrain pair configuration energy only and exclude kinetic momentum energy.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-td-retained-hard-core-zero-beta-preparation",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Retained hard-core continuation at zero inverse temperature",
        "statement": "For the pair potential $\\varphi$ of [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]], let $B=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^d:\\varphi(x)=+\\infty\\}$. A finite configuration is hard-admissible if every pair displacement avoids $B$. For $z>0$, define the retained-admissibility zero-inverse-temperature free-wall weight of $\\omega$ to be\n$$w_0(\\omega)=z^{N(\\omega)}\\mathbf 1_{\\{H_{N(\\omega)}(\\omega)<\\infty\\}}.$$\nIts finite-volume partition sum, when finite, is $\\Xi_{\\Lambda}^{\\mathrm{ret},0}(z)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}\\int w_0(\\omega)\\,\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega)$, using [[post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure]]. This definition sets the weight of a forbidden configuration to zero; it does not assign a value to the undefined product $0\\cdot(+\\infty)$. When $B=\\varnothing$ the measure is the unrestricted Poisson activity model; for a nonempty hard core it is generally not Poisson. The convention is explicit model data and carries no statement about an arbitrary zero-width energy shell.\n\nFor a periodic cell of side $L$ in dimensionless coordinates, use the exact image energy $H_{L,\\mathrm{per}}$ of [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]]. Its periodic retained-admissibility weight is\n$$w_{L,0}^{\\mathrm{per}}(\\omega)=z^{N(\\omega)}\\mathbf1_{\\{H_{L,\\mathrm{per}}(\\omega)<+\\infty\\}}=z^{N(\\omega)}\\mathbf1_{\\{\\varphi(y-x)<+\\infty\\text{ for every }x\\in\\omega,\\ y\\in\\omega^{\\mathrm{per}},\\ y\\ne x\\}}.$$\nThus every forbidden pair between distinct particles and every forbidden periodic-image pair, including a particle with one of its own nontrivial images, receives zero weight. When finite, the corresponding partition sum is $\\Xi_{L}^{\\mathrm{ret},0,\\mathrm{per}}(z)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}\\int w_{L,0}^{\\mathrm{per}}\\,d\\mu_{\\Lambda_L,N}$. This is the periodic retained-admissibility convention; it is not an unrestricted zero-beta limit of a shell law.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation",
          "post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Hans-Otto Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction, Probability Theory and Related Fields 99 (1994), 171–195",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Konstantin K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part SM: Statistical Mechanics (2024 author-hosted lecture notes)",
              "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Essential_Graduate_Physics_-_Statistical_Mechanics_%28Likharev%29/03%3A_Ideal_and_Not-So-Ideal_Gases/3.01%3A_Ideal_classical_gas"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "Finite-volume positional models with forbidden pair separations; this is a declared beta-zero hard-admissibility continuation, not a claim about all infinite-temperature physical preparations.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-equilibrium-ensemble-sampling",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Finite-volume equilibrium ensemble sampling",
        "statement": "For a specified finite region and free or periodic boundary preparation, take $T>0$, $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$ with the SI conventions of [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]], and the corresponding positional energy. Write $H_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{free}}=H_N$ for a free region and, for a periodic cube $\\Lambda_L$, write $H_{\\Lambda_L,N}^{\\mathrm{per}}=H_{L,\\mathrm{per}}$; in each case $N=N(\\omega)$. Set $\\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{fin}}=\\{\\omega\\in\\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,N}:H_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{bc}}(\\omega)<+\\infty\\}$. This set is measurable because the pair potential is measurable and the periodic energy is a countable sum of measurable image terms. On it $H_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{bc}}$ is real-valued. Specialize the measurable-model canonical preparation of [[post-td-canonical-sampling]] to this set with reference measure $\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}$ restricted to it; adopt\n$$Z_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{bc}}=\\int_{\\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{fin}}}e^{-\\beta H_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{bc}}(\\omega)}\\,\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega),\\qquad P_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{can}}(d\\omega)=\\frac{\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal C_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{fin}}}(\\omega)e^{-\\beta H_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{bc}}(\\omega)}}{Z_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{bc}}}\\,\\mu_{\\Lambda,N}(d\\omega),$$\nwhenever $0<Z_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{bc}}<\\infty$. Define the Boltzmann factor to be zero on configurations with energy $+\\infty$; thus no value of an exponential at $+\\infty$ is needed. Separately adopt the grand-canonical law at activity $z>0$ to have weights $z^Ne^{-\\beta H_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{bc}}}$ on the finite-energy part of each sector and zero elsewhere, relative to the same indistinguishable positional measures. Its normalizer is $\\Xi_\\Lambda(z,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}z^NZ_{\\Lambda,N}^{\\mathrm{bc}}$ when finite. Here $z$ is dimensionless relative to the reference intensity in [[post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure]], and $\\alpha=\\log z$.\n\nFor the controlled positional shell of [[def-td-controlled-fixed-n-energy-shell]], adopt the conditional measure $M_n$ only when its shell has positive finite reference measure. These are probability-law preparations used to define the model. They are not derived from Hamiltonian trajectories, and they do not assert that a laboratory apparatus reaches any of them. The inverse-temperature-zero hard-core continuation is governed separately by [[def-td-retained-hard-core-zero-beta-preparation]].",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation",
          "post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure",
          "def-td-controlled-fixed-n-energy-shell",
          "post-td-canonical-sampling"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-controlled-fixed-n-energy-shell": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-canonical-sampling": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics (author-hosted Cambridge lecture notes)",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S1"
            },
            {
              "title": "Konstantin K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, Part SM: Statistical Mechanics (2024 author-hosted lecture notes)",
              "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Essential_Graduate_Physics_-_Statistical_Mechanics_%28Likharev%29/02%3A_Principles_of_Physical_Statistics/2.04%3A_Canonical_ensemble_and_the_Gibbs_distribution"
            },
            {
              "title": "Hans-Otto Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction, Probability Theory and Related Fields 99 (1994), 171–195",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "Adopted canonical, grand-canonical, and positional microcanonical preparations of a classical pair-potential model in a stated finite region; no dynamical relaxation, ergodicity, or empirical thermalization is asserted.",
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "library": "physics"
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-grand-variational-phase-uniqueness",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Selected grand-variational phase uniqueness",
        "statement": "Fix the selected stable regularly tempered pair model with Borel-measurable $\\widehat\\varphi=\\varphi/E_0$, $T>0$, $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$ with the SI conventions of [[def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation]], and activity $z>0$. For the superstable branch, require measurable even pair potentials $\\varphi_s,\\varphi_r$ with $\\varphi=\\varphi_s+\\varphi_r$, where $\\varphi_s$ is stable in joules and $\\varphi_r(x)\\geq0$ joules for every $x$. Require constants $\\epsilon>0$ joules and $r_0>0$ in dimensionless coordinates such that $\\varphi_r(x)\\geq\\epsilon$ whenever $\\|x\\|_\\infty\\leq r_0$, where $\\|x\\|_\\infty=\\max_j|x_j|$. These are exactly the stable-plus-nonnegative-core conditions of [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]] for $\\widehat\\varphi$, after energy conversion; this is an additional model hypothesis, not a consequence of stability alone.\n\nUnder the Axiom of Choice, use the global measurable configuration space $\\Omega$ of [[def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference]], consisting of locally finite simple configurations with sigma-algebra generated by bounded-Borel count maps. For $a\\in\\mathbb R^d$, let $T_a\\omega(A)=\\omega(A-a)$ be its measurable translation. A probability law $P$ on $\\Omega$ is stationary exactly when $P\\circ T_a^{-1}=P$ for every $a\\in\\mathbb R^d$. For a bounded Borel region $A$, equip its finite simple configuration set $\\mathcal C_A$ with $\\mathcal F_A^{\\rm count}=\\sigma(N_B:B\\subseteq A\\text{ Borel})$, without completing it. On this space $r_A(\\omega)=\\omega\\cap A$ is measurable: each generating count satisfies $N_B\\circ r_A=N_B$, a bounded-Borel global count. Write $P_A=P\\circ r_A^{-1}$ on this exact count sigma-algebra for every stationary $P$. This assertion does not extend $P_A$ to the completed quotient sigma-algebra. The reference $Q_A=Q\\circ r_A^{-1}$ is the restriction of the same unit-intensity law $Q$; by [[thm-tdmath-poisson-reference-count-properties]] and the count-generated restriction sigma-algebra, it equals the restriction to $\\mathcal F_A^{\\rm count}$ of the finite-sector law $e^{-|A|}\\sum_{N\\geq0}\\mu_{A,N}$ of [[post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure]]. This count sigma-algebra is contained in the completed quotient sigma-algebra: in each sector, the pullback of $N_B$ is $\\sum_{j=1}^N\\mathbf1_B(x_j)$, a Borel function, so each count generator is quotient measurable. Thus the stated restriction of the positional measure is defined. Relative entropy and the restrictions below always use $\\mathcal F_A^{\\rm count}$, exactly as in the mathematical global supplier, rather than an unproved completion for arbitrary $P$. Indeed, a finite Borel partition of $A$ has multinomial counts under each fixed-$N$ Lebesgue sector, and summing these sector measures with the factor $e^{-|A|}$ gives independent Poisson counts of the corresponding volumes. Use the finite-region relative entropy $D(P_0\\|Q_0)$ of [[def-tdmath-finite-region-relative-entropy]]: if $P_0\\ll Q_0$ and $h=dP_0/dQ_0$, then $D=\\int h\\log h\\,dQ_0$ with $0\\log0=0$; otherwise $D=+\\infty$. Let $C=[-1/2,1/2)^d$ and define $\\rho(P)=E_PN_C$. On stationary laws for which the finite limits exist, define\n$$I(P)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}\\frac{D(P_{\\Lambda_L}\\|Q_{\\Lambda_L})}{|\\Lambda_L|},\\qquad \\Phi(P)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}\\frac{E_P[H_{\\Lambda_L}^{\\mathrm{free}}]}{|\\Lambda_L|}.$$\nFor $\\beta>0$, use the stationary laws with finite $I(P)$ and $\\Phi(P)$ and adopt, at the selected $(\\varphi,\\beta,z)$, that\n$$\\operatorname*{arg\\,min}_{P}\\{I(P)+\\beta\\Phi(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z\\}=\\{P^\\ast\\}.$$\n\nFor the $\\beta=0$ branch, require a nonempty hard-core displacement set $B=\\{x:\\varphi(x)=+\\infty\\}$ and use [[def-td-retained-hard-core-zero-beta-preparation]]. A global configuration is hard-admissible when no pair of its distinct points has displacement in $B$. Restrict to stationary laws assigning probability one to hard-admissible configurations and having finite $I(P)$; adopt, at the selected $z$, that\n$$\\operatorname*{arg\\,min}_{P}\\{I(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z\\}=\\{P^\\ast\\}.$$\nThese are two explicit parameter-specific physical model restrictions. They do not assert uniqueness for every activity or temperature, do not apply at coexistence when the minimizer set has several elements, and do not infer uniqueness from thermodynamic smoothness.",
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-td-stable-tempered-continuum-preparation",
          "post-td-classical-indistinguishable-particle-measure",
          "def-td-retained-hard-core-zero-beta-preparation",
          "post-td-equilibrium-ensemble-sampling",
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference",
          "thm-tdmath-poisson-reference-count-properties",
          "def-tdmath-finite-region-relative-entropy"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
